const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:"cat > /tmp/exposed-delta-architecture/revised-c.md <<'EOF'
# Revised recommendation: declaration-ID delta, complete snapshot resolution

Daily synchronization should operate only on declarations. Member records have a separate, infrequent lifecycle and do not belong in this command. Remove member enumeration, freshness checks, metadata fetches, and member backfills from daily sync. `MemberId` is only the source lookup key carried by declaration discovery/hydration; it does not authorize a member API call.

## Acquisition and scope

Use the committed declaration snapshot as the base and freeze an overlapping inclusive publication/update date window. Query both publication and update windows, union results by declaration/interest ID, and treat those filtered responses solely as discovery receipts. For every in-cohort discovered ID, fetch its unfiltered complete record set, including expired records and separate children. The observed interest 14513 response proves date-filtered results can omit historical register versions; only unfiltered hydration can replace that ID's evidence safely.

Define the cohort explicitly from the committed declaration snapshot's member IDs, with an optional configured initial cohort on bootstrap. A discovered record whose `MemberId` is outside that cohort is recorded in the run report as out-of-scope and does not alter the declaration snapshot. This is a scope decision, not a fetch failure. For in-scope records, missing pages, malformed payloads, conflicting duplicate IDs, ownership changes, or failed hydration fail the run; never convert these into omission or success. Do not fetch member metadata to decide scope.

Merge each complete hydrated ID set into the retained full raw snapshot by stable declaration ID, validating that the record's member agrees with prior ownership. Keep prior evidence when a response omits a record; omission is not proof of deletion. Preserve parent and child records, and all unchanged declarations. Seal only when every in-scope ID is fully traversed. Store immutable partition hashes, discovery receipts, query/window policy, cohort, and base generation; do not relabel reused evidence as freshly retrieved.

## Interpretation and publication

Run existing global cleaning, resolution, and grouping against the complete merged snapshot. This is required even with declaration-ID acquisition: parent changes can affect unchanged children, and aliases or identity components can change across the corpus. Publish the full resolved projection atomically. Do not scope resolution/publication to changed IDs.

Keep the existing repository transaction as publication owner: expected-base generation check, declaration load, group refresh, snapshot/run reference, and checkpoint advance commit together. Exact committed run replay returns its prior result; stale bases rebuild from the newer snapshot. Empty scans advance coverage without changing evidence or identities. Artifact storage must outlive scheduler instances and retain every partition referenced by the committed snapshot.

## Domain sketch

```rust
struct DeclarationCohort { member_ids: BTreeSet<MemberId> }
struct DiscoveryWindow { published: SourceDateRange, updated: SourceDateRange }
struct DiscoveredIds { receipts: Vec<DiscoveryReceipt>, ids: BTreeSet<InterestId> }
struct InScopeIds { ids: BTreeSet<InterestId> }
struct HydratedInterest { id: InterestId, records: Vec<RawDeclaration> }
struct CompleteDeclarationCapture { /* verified merged snapshot + manifest */ }

impl DeclarationSyncService {
    async fn sync(&self, mode: SyncMode) -> Result<SyncReport, EntityIngestionError>;
    async fn resume(&self, run: IngestionKey) -> Result<SyncReport, EntityIngestionError>;
}
```

The Parliament adapter owns query parsing and unfiltered hydration; the domain service applies cohort scope and completeness rules; the artifact adapter owns sealed immutable partitions; existing clean/resolve/load services remain canonical. Add reversible migrations for declaration snapshot generation, discovery checkpoint, and run publication metadata. Do not add daily member state or duplicate member freshness policy.

## Tradeoffs and risks

This reduces remote acquisition to changed declaration IDs while retaining full global interpretation; no runtime savings are claimed until measured. It adds ID-level merge complexity, ownership validation, and durable snapshot artifacts compared with changed-member rescans. Discovery coverage remains uncertain until the source's date filters, update semantics, and pagination behavior are verified. Offset pagination has no documented snapshot guarantee, so overlap and periodic declaration reconciliation reduce risk but cannot prove completeness. Deletion requires an explicit source retirement signal or reviewed reconciliation policy; omission and empty results never delete. Full global resolution can still change noncompany funder IDs because current identity keys include ingestion context; stable IDs need a separate reconciliation design.
EOF
wc -w /tmp/exposed-delta-architecture/revised-c.md
